Converting MEL to Python (Part 1)
- Maya heavily dependant on MEL
- when developing your own tools, it is likely that you will need to go through default functionality and copy it
- UI actions are all executed with MEL and get output to the editor output
    - Even action such as 'New File' or 'Open Recent' get executed as MEL
- in MEL some flags can have multiple parameters which are space separated
- In python when there are multiple parameters they are passed in as a list []
    - this includes names ["pCube1", "pCube2", "pCube3"]
- MEL flags start with a dash '-', python they are keyword arguments (query=True)
    - if MEL flag takes no arguments, in python it will take a boolean (True/False)

Converting MEL to Python (Part 2)
- syntax from MEL to python a bit different
- MEL (command flags names)
- Python (command names parameters) - NOTE names are strings and require quotations ""
- Convertion is imperative for effective maya python development

Core Data Types
- everything in python is an object (can be assigned to a variable)
- lists are like arrays in other languages, they are denoted with square brackets []
- tuple is an immutable list, denoted with regular parenthesis ()
    - you can copy all values and edit values but this will cause a new object to be created
- Type(object) will tell you what type an object is

Variables
- variables are custom named representations of objects in your script
- variables allow you to refer to and track objects you have created

Literals
- in contrast to a variable that can change a literal is fixed representation of a value in source code
- 20, 3.14 are literals
- literals cannot be used as variable names

Maya Commands: getAttr
- allows you to get any attribute from an object in the scene
- uses string notation
- period indicates hierarchy
- can use long or short name for attributes
    - cmds.getAttr("World2.visibility") OR cmds.getAttr("World2.v")
- using getAttr for transform information is common and quick (tx, ty, tz, rx, ry, rz, sx, sy, sz)
- Finding attribute names
    - select an object and change that attribute and it will output in MEL showing you the name
    - go to (Channel Box/Layer Editor > Object > Channel Controls > Channel Names > Long/Short)
- Locked: (flag) getAttr can test to see if attribute is locked

Maya Commands: setAttr
- allows you to modify the attribute values on objects

Importing Modules
- bring other python libraries into your script for use

Pass By Reference
- when passing literals they are passed by value (copies), changes to them in a function will not propagate
- in python most things are passed by reference, adjustments in functions propagate




